

#   Explain the four pillars of OOP with real project examples.

    The four pillars of OOP (Object-Oriented Programming) are:
    1. Encapsulation
    2. Inheritance
    3. Polymorphism
    4. Abstraction

    1. Encapsulation
    Definition: Encapsulation means wrapping data (variables) and methods into a single unit (class) and restricting
    direct access to the data.

    We achieve this using:
    - private variables.
    - Public getters and setters.
    - Controlled access through methods.
    Real project example: Payment Service

    Suppose we have a payment transaction. We should not allow other classes to directly modify its payment status.

    public class Payment {

        private Long paymentId;
        private double amount;
        private String status;

        public String getStatus() {
            return status;
        }

        public void updateStatus(String status) {
            if (status != null &&
                (status.equals("SUCCESS") ||
                 status.equals("FAILED"))) {
                this.status = status;
            }
        }
    }


    Here:
    - Variables are private.
    - Other classes cannot directly modify the payment status.
    - We expose controlled access through methods.
    - We can validate the status before updating it.
    Interview explanation:

    "In my payment module, I used encapsulation to protect transaction data. For example, payment status was private,
    and we exposed controlled methods to update it. This helped maintain data integrity and prevent invalid state
    changes."

    2. Inheritance
    Definition: Inheritance allows one class to acquire the properties and behaviours of another class.

    We use the extends keyword.

    Real project example: Common payment functionality

    Suppose we have multiple payment types, such as card payment and UPI payment. Both require common payment-related
    functionality.
    class Payment {

        protected double amount;

        public void validatePayment() {
            System.out.println("Validating payment");
        }
    }

    class CardPayment extends Payment {

        public void processCardPayment() {
            validatePayment();
            System.out.println("Processing card payment");
        }
    }

    class UpiPayment extends Payment {

        public void processUpiPayment() {
            validatePayment();
            System.out.println("Processing UPI payment");
        }
    }


    Here:
    - Payment is the parent class.
    - CardPayment and UpiPayment are child classes.
    - Both reuse the common validation functionality.
    Interview explanation:

    "In my payment module, inheritance can be used to reuse common payment functionality. For example, different
    payment implementations can inherit common validation logic from a base payment class, reducing code duplication."

    Note: In real Spring Boot projects, composition and interfaces are often preferred over deep inheritance
    hierarchies.

    3. Polymorphism
    Definition: Polymorphism means one interface or method can have multiple forms of behaviour.

    There are two types:
    - Compile-time polymorphism (method overloading).
    - Runtime polymorphism (method overriding).
    Real project example: Multiple payment methods

    Imagine users can pay through cards or UPI.
    interface PaymentService {

        void processPayment(double amount);
    }

    class CardPaymentService implements PaymentService {

        @Override
        public void processPayment(double amount) {
            System.out.println("Processing card payment: " + amount);
        }
    }

    class UpiPaymentService implements PaymentService {

        @Override
        public void processPayment(double amount) {
            System.out.println("Processing UPI payment: " + amount);
        }
    }


    Now:
    PaymentService payment;

    payment = new CardPaymentService();
    payment.processPayment(1000);

    payment = new UpiPaymentService();
    payment.processPayment(2000);


    Output:
    Processing card payment: 1000.0
    Processing UPI payment: 2000.0


    The same method processPayment() behaves differently depending on the actual object.

    Interview explanation:

    "In my payment module, polymorphism allows us to process different payment methods using a common interface. For example, CardPaymentService and UpiPaymentService implement the same PaymentService interface but provide their own implementations. This makes the system flexible and easier to extend."
    4. Abstraction
    Definition: Abstraction means hiding implementation details and exposing only essential functionality to the user.

    We achieve abstraction using:
    - Interfaces.
    - Abstract classes.
    Real project example: Payment gateway integration

    When a user makes a payment, the application doesn't need to know the internal implementation of every payment gateway.

    It only needs to know how to initiate a payment.
    interface PaymentGateway {

        void initiatePayment(double amount);

        void refundPayment(String transactionId);
    }


    Implementation:
    class RazorpayGateway implements PaymentGateway {

        public void initiatePayment(double amount) {
            System.out.println("Initiating Razorpay payment");
        }

        public void refundPayment(String transactionId) {
            System.out.println("Processing Razorpay refund");
        }
    }


    Another implementation:
    class StripeGateway implements PaymentGateway {

        public void initiatePayment(double amount) {
            System.out.println("Initiating Stripe payment");
        }

        public void refundPayment(String transactionId) {
            System.out.println("Processing Stripe refund");
        }
    }


    The calling service interacts with the PaymentGateway interface without worrying about the internal gateway
    implementation.

    Interview explanation:

    "In my payment project, abstraction can be applied through a PaymentGateway interface. It exposes methods like
    initiatePayment() and refundPayment(), while hiding the internal implementation details of individual payment gateways."